[Puzzle "Punish the pawn grab"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "9.dxc5"]
[End "9.dxc5"]
[Hint "Black's king is still on e8. Find a move that opens lines toward it."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 c5 6. Be3 Qb6 7. Nc3 Qxb2? 8. Qb1 {Offers a queen trade.} Qb6? {Black refuses the trade, but the king is still in the center.}
(8... Qxb1+ {The best reply: Black takes the trade.} 9. Rxb1 c4 10. Rxb7 {White wins back the pawn. Stockfish: +0.8, slightly better for White.})
9. dxc5! {[%cal Gd4c5,Ge2b5] Takes a pawn, and after 9...Bxc5, Bb5+ catches Black's king in the center.}
(9. Bb5+!? {Playable: the check first. White keeps an edge, but a smaller one.} 9... Nd7 10. dxc5 Qc7 {Stockfish: +0.8, slightly better for White.})
9... Bxc5 10. Bb5+ {[%cal Gb5e8]} Kd8 {Blocking with a knight loses more.} 11. Na4 Qa5+ 12. Bd2 Qc7 {Black's king is stuck in the center and can no longer castle. Stockfish: +1.6, clearly better for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Bishop check, knight fork"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "7.Bxf7+"]
[End "8.Ne5+"]
[Hint "f7 is guarded only by the king, and nothing guards the bishop on g4."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. f3 exf3 5. Nxf3 Bg4 6. Bc4 Nf6? 7. Bxf7+! {[%cal Gc4f7] Gives the bishop to pull Black's king onto f7.}
(7. Ne5! {Also strong: the knight hits the bishop on g4 and the pawn on f7 at once.} 7... Be6 8. Bxe6 fxe6 {Black's e-pawns are doubled and the f-file is open toward the king. Stockfish: +1.7, clearly better for White.})
7... Kxf7 8. Ne5+! {[%cal Ge5g4] Check, and the knight also attacks the bishop on g4.} Kg8 9. Nxg4 {White has won back the piece, so material is level, but Black's king can no longer castle and the rook on h8 is shut in. Stockfish: +1.8, clearly better for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Save the bishop"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Save the piece"]
[Solve "6...h6"]
[End "6...h6"]
[Hint "White threatens h4-h5. Where will your bishop go then?"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6! {[%cal Gg6h7] Makes room on h7. After h5, the bishop drops back there and stays alive.}
(6... e6? {Natural, but too slow.} 7. h5 Bf5 8. Nxf5 exf5 {The knight takes the bishop. Black's f-pawns are doubled and the pawn on f5 is weak, while White keeps both bishops. Stockfish: +2.1, winning for White. The score counts weaknesses, not material: the pieces are still level.})
(6... h5!? {Playable, but weaker: it stops h5 for good, but gives away the g5 square.} 7. c3 e6 8. Nh3 {The knight heads for f4 or g5. Stockfish: +1.0, clearly better for White, against +0.3 after 6...h6.})
(6... Nf6? 7. h5 Be4 8. Rh4 {[%cal Gh4e4] The rook joins the hunt for the bishop. Stockfish: +1.7, clearly better for White.})
7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 {The bishop is safe on h7. This is the main line of the Classical. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Trade first"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Avoid the trap"]
[Solve "8...Bxd3"]
[End "8...Bxd3"]
[Hint "White's bishop on d3 attacks yours. What happens if White is the one who takes?"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. Bd3 Bxd3! {Take first. White has to recapture with the queen, and Black's pawns stay healthy.}
(8... e6? {Develops, but lets White take on g6.} 9. Bxg6 fxg6 10. Qd3 {Black's pawns are doubled, e6 is weak, and the queen eyes g6. Stockfish: +2.3, winning for White.})
(8... Bh7? 9. Bxh7 Rxh7 10. c4 {The rook on h7 is out of play. Stockfish: +1.6, clearly better for White.})
9. Qxd3 e6 {The usual setup: ...Ngf6, ...Be7 and castling come next. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.}
*

[Puzzle "Shut the diagonal"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Avoid the trap"]
[Solve "6...e6"]
[End "6...e6"]
[Hint "White's bishop on c4 and knight on f3 both eye f7. Close the line before developing."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. f3 exf3 5. Nxf3 Bg4 6. Bc4 e6! {[%cal Gc4f7] [%csl Ge6] Blocks the bishop's diagonal to f7. Now Black can develop the kingside in peace.}
(6... Nf6? {Natural, but it walks into a trick.} 7. Bxf7+! Kxf7 8. Ne5+ Kg8 9. Nxg4 {The knight check forks the king and the bishop on g4. White has won back the pawn, and Black's king can no longer castle. Stockfish: +1.8, clearly better for White. And if 7.Ne5 instead, 7...Bxd1?? allows 8.Bxf7 mate.})
(6... Bxf3!? {Playable: it also avoids the trap, but gives up the bishop pair and Black's edge.} 7. Qxf3 e6 8. Be3 {White's bishops and quick development make up for the pawn. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
7. O-O Be7 8. h3 Bh5 {Black is a pawn up with a solid position. Stockfish: −1.0, clearly better for Black.}
*

[Puzzle "Kick the knight"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "5.e5"]
[End "5.e5"]
[Hint "Black's knight on f6 has few good squares. Chase it with a pawn."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 dxe4 4. fxe4 Nf6? 5. e5! {[%cal Ge5f6] Kicks the knight. Its best square is d5, where c4 kicks it again.}
(5. Nc3? {Develops, but lets Black hit back in the center.} 5... e5 6. dxe5 Qxd1+ 7. Nxd1 Nxe4 {Black wins back the pawn and the queens are off. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.})
5... Nd5 6. c4 Nc7 7. Nc3 {Black's knight has been chased to c7, and White has a big center. Stockfish: +1.4, clearly better for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Check on the weak diagonal"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "5...Qh4+"]
[End "5...Qh4+"]
[Hint "f3 opened the diagonal from h4 to e1. Which check also eyes e4?"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 5. dxe5? Qh4+! {[%cal Gh4e1,Gh4e4] Check, and the queen eyes the loose pawn on e4.}
(5... Qxd1+? {Trades queens and lets White off the hook.} 6. Kxd1 Be6 7. Be3 {White is a pawn up for now and Black has no attack. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.})
(5... Bb4+!? {Playable: also a check, but 6.c3 blocks it and the attack fades.} 6. c3 Qh4+ 7. Kd2 {Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
6. Kd2 {The best defense.}
(6. g3? Qxe4+ {Forks the king and the rook on h1.} 7. Qe2 Qxh1 {Black is a rook up. Stockfish: −4.4, winning for Black.})
(6. Ke2? Qxe4+ 7. Be3 Bc5 {Black's pieces swarm around the exposed king. Stockfish: −2.9, winning for Black.})
6... Qf4+ 7. Ke1 Qxe4+ 8. Qe2 Bf5 {Black has won back the pawn, White's king can no longer castle, and the pawn on e5 is weak. Stockfish: −1.1, clearly better for Black.}
*

[Puzzle "Mate on f7"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Checkmate in 1"]
[Solve "9.Bxf7#"]
[Hint "The knight on e5 covers d7. Where can Black's king go?"]

1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Qe2 Nxe4 6. Qxe4 Nd7 7. Bc4 Nf6 8. Ne5 Nxe4?? 9. Bxf7# {[%cal Gc4f7,Ge5f7,Ge5d7] Mate. The king can't take on f7, because the knight on e5 guards it, and the knight covers d7 too. Taking the queen was poisoned.}
*

[Puzzle "Mate on the dark squares"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Checkmate in 2"]
[Solve "6...Bf2+"]
[End "7...Qe3#"]
[Hint "Your queen and bishop both aim at f2 and e3. Start with a check."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 e5 4. dxe5 Qb6 5. exd5 Bc5 6. Ne2?? Bf2+! {[%cal Gc5f2] Check. The king has only one square.}
7. Kd2 Qe3# {[%cal Gb6e3] Mate. The queen covers c3 and d3, the bishop on f2 covers e1 and guards the queen, and White's own pieces block the rest.}
*

[Puzzle "The smothered mate"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Checkmate in 1"]
[Solve "6.Nd6#"]
[Hint "Black's e7 pawn is pinned by your queen. Which square can it no longer guard?"]

1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Qe2 Nbd7?? 6. Nd6# {[%cal Ge4d6] Smothered mate. The e7 pawn is pinned and can't take the knight, and Black's own pieces block every escape square.}
(6. Nxf6+? {Trades knights and misses the mate.} 6... Nxf6 7. d4 {Stockfish: −0.2, equal.})
*

[Puzzle "A pinned knight jumps"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "9.Ne5"]
[End "9.Ne5"]
[Hint "Your knight on f3 is pinned to the queen, but it can still move. Where does it hit the bishop and f7 together?"]

1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Qe2 Nxe4 6. Qxe4 Nd7 7. Bc4 Nf6 8. Qe2 Bg4? 9. Ne5! {[%cal Ge5g4,Gc4f7] The pinned knight moves anyway, with two threats: Nxg4, and Bxf7 mate, since the knight covers d7. If 9...Bxe2, 10.Bxf7 is mate.}
(9. Bxf7+!? {Also wins material, but less.} 9... Kxf7 10. Ne5+ Ke8 11. Nxg4 Nxg4 12. Qxg4 {White is a pawn up. Stockfish: +1.9, clearly better for White, against +2.6 after 9.Ne5.})
(9. h3? {Too slow: the bishop simply retreats.} 9... Bh5 10. d4 e6 {Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
9... Be6 {The only way to stop Bxf7 mate and save the bishop.}
(9... Qd4? 10. Bxf7+ Kd8 11. f3 {The bishop on g4 is trapped. Stockfish: +3.9, winning for White.})
10. Bxe6 fxe6 11. O-O {Material is level, but Black's pawns on e6 and e7 are weak and the king is stuck in the center. Stockfish: +2.6, winning for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Queen to h5"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "8.Qh5"]
[End "8.Qh5"]
[Hint "f7 is defended only by the king. Add an attacker with a threat."]

1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6?! 6. h4 h6 7. Ne5 Bh7? 8. Qh5! {[%cal Gh5f7,Ge5f7] Threatens Qxf7 mate.}
(8. Qf3!? {Playable: it also threatens Qxf7 mate, but Black defends with ...Nf6 and White's edge is smaller.} 8... Nf6 9. Qb3 Qd5 10. Qxb7 Qxe5+ 11. Be2 e6 {Stockfish: +0.8, slightly better for White.})
(8. Bc4!? {Playable: good, but slower.} 8... e6 9. Qe2 Qe7 10. d4 {Stockfish: +0.9, slightly better for White.})
(8. d4?! 8... Nd7 9. Bd2 {The chance has gone. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
8... g6 {Black's best defense.}
(8... Qd5? 9. Bc4 {Hits the queen and adds a third attacker on f7. Stockfish: +5.4, winning for White.})
(8... Bg6 9. Nxg6 {Black must recapture with the f-pawn, wrecking the kingside. Stockfish: +3.0, winning for White.})
9. Qf3 {[%cal Gf3f7,Ge5f7] The queen steps out of the attack and threatens Qxf7 mate again.}
(9. Bc4 {Also strong: the bishop adds a second attacker on f7, and 9...gxh5?? 10.Bxf7# is mate.} 9... e6 10. Qe2 {Stockfish: +1.8, clearly better for White.})
9... Nf6 10. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3f7] Now the queen hits b7 and f7 at once.} Qd5 11. Qxb7 Qxe5+ 12. Be2 {White has given the knight on e5 but will take the rook on a8. Stockfish: +1.8, clearly better for White.}
*

[Puzzle "The sacrifice on f7"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Tactics"]
[Solve "8.Nxf7"]
[End "8.Nxf7"]
[Hint "Your knight on g5 is attacked. Move it with the biggest threat."]

1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Bc4 Ngf6 6. Neg5 e6 7. Qe2 h6?? 8. Nxf7! {[%cal Gg5f7] The knight takes f7 and forks the queen and the rook on h8.}
(8. Ne4? {Retreats, and the chance is gone.} 8... Nxe4 9. Qxe4 Nf6 {Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
(8. Nxe6? {The wrong sacrifice.} 8... fxe6 9. O-O Nb6 {White has given a knight for a pawn. Stockfish: −1.7, clearly better for Black.})
8... Qc7
(8... Kxf7 9. Qxe6+ Kg6 10. Bd3+ Kh5 11. Qh3# {Mate: the king was dragged into the open.})
9. Nxh8 {White wins the rook and a pawn. Stockfish: +7.0, winning for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Block the queen"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Defend the king"]
[Solve "4...g6"]
[End "4...g6"]
[Hint "White's queen and bishop both aim at f7. Shut out one of them."]

1. e4 c6 2. Bc4 d5 3. Bb3 dxe4 4. Qh5 g6! {[%cal Rh5f7] Kicks the queen off the h5-f7 line. Black keeps the extra pawn.}
(4... e6! {Also good: it shuts out the bishop instead.} 5. Nc3 a5 6. Nxe4 {Stockfish: −0.5, slightly better for Black.})
(4... Nf6? {Develops, but walks into a check on f7.} 5. Qxf7+ Kd7 6. Nc3 {Black's king is stuck in the open. Stockfish: +2.2, winning for White.})
(4... Nh6!? {Playable: it guards f7, but the knight is offside.} 5. Nc3 Nd7 6. Nxe4 {White has won back the pawn. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.})
5. Qe2 Nf6 6. Nc3 {Black is a pawn up. Stockfish: −0.8, slightly better for Black.}
*
